e Shura Shura is were  and to no sound to try and shore up the  investment in the future to the UK as a  forward business secretary can you give  us a bit of insight into the kind of  thing that the government would have  been saying well I've been trying for  the the last year or so to find out  exactly what those assurances were of  course there is a limit to what  government can  because of state aid rules but I'm quite  sure that they had found a way of  offering this and money to invest here  with dear mother  well money guarantees support for  research they're all kind of different  ways that you can do this within the  rules but the the reason that I think  that they focused on this sound is that  Nissan are symbolically massively  important if we go back mrs. Thatcher's  debt is the big turnaround in British  economy occurred when mrs. Thatcher  persuaded Miss Anne to come and invest  in the Northeast very much based on  Europe being part of the single market  as it as it subsequently become and it's  become a kind of totem of the success of  British manufacturing based on so if if  they pull it I mean that is a massive  psychological blow as well as an  economic blow to the Northeast and  you're talking about the uncertainty  about brexit and the fear of no deal we  don't know what the situation is of  course I'm particularly Nissan and but  the fear of no deal is certainly  something that businesses are talking  about a lot so my question to you is why  is it that the Lib Dems are still  pursuing the unicorn of a second  referendum when it's pretty clear that  there's no majority in parliament Fred  there's only two months until bright  said would it be better if you should be  focusing your attention on trying to get  a brexit deal over the line that  actually does manage to safeguard jobs  as much as possible well it isn't a  unicorn I mean this is a question of  timing I mean I think what is going to  happen is that Theresa May will bring  back her deal to Parliament I think it's  very unlikely to pass it might but I  think it's very unlikely at the  Parliament certainly is going to reject  No Deal and half the cabinet's as well  well they haven't so far majority to  reject the farm because the majority of  the options haven't crystallized I think  we may get to a point and it will be a  few weeks from here where the government  may itself come to the conclusion that  the only way of getting its deal to  progress is actually to take it to the  country I think Buddhists can change  very quickly in Parliament once they see  what the options are the idea of going  back to the public and giving them the  choice  of the government's deal or some  variants of it as against remaining in  the European Union will soon seem a very  attractive one and I think it is  potentially very live though currently  it is a question of timing now you've  said that you'll step down when brexit  is either stopped all resolved and  there's reports in the papers today in  the mail and Sunday that the government  is planning to potentially call a snap  general election in June so if that does  happen will you leave the Lib Dems into  it quite probably yes I have said indeed  I said I had a series of tasks which  indeed were to complete the the brexit  argument it may not be resolved by the  end of May or certainly not by the end  of March a two-carat take us through  local elections and into an early  general election so I think it's it's  very likely yes but we there are so many  uncertainties at the moment and I mean  some people will be thinking that you  know you've obviously waited a while to  to get this job and as the leader of the  Liberal Democrats but at the same time  you've struggled to really make an  impact haven't you I think we've made a  lot of progress actually I mean if you  look at what's happening on the ground  as opposed to speculation about the  future polls don't suggest that they do  they well the polls do actually if there  have been a lot of local polls within  which we do extremely well the national  polls are not spectacularly good but  they're certainly better than they have  been for the last eight years and the  most recent one I saw the last weekend  suggested that if the labour party stick  to their present course we finish up in  the 20s mid twenties I mean there is a  lot of optimism the party we're at  record levels of membership people in  our joining us in disgust at the Labour  Party and I'm very positive I think  we've made real progress and if there is  another election will lose surprisingly  well  there also I mean there's there's  constant chatter isn't there about the  possibility of a new party being formed  and with speculation that imported into  the minute-hand vacatur the center  ground there's a story in The Observer  about its day saying six Labour MPs are  effectively poised to quit the party and  do just that so have you been approached  by MPs from from other part  yes and they have been I mean it's well  reported for months that there is a  actually much bigger group of Labour MPs  not just six who are deeply unhappy with  Corbin's leadership and don't see  they've spoken to you about it then oh  yes indeed  well this conservatives as well as so  it's not just labour party what do they  set you I think as of now they're  basically working with us just stop  wrecks it to try and get people's vote  there is a very strong group of people  working as a team to try and deliver  that I think when the brexit issue is  resolved or one way or the other  I think they will reconsider their  position in the labour party I can't  predict exactly what will happen but I  think there's a real chance of a  significant group breaking away and if  that happens we will work with them in  some form and what does that mean then  does that mean that they will join the  Liberal Democrats or does it actually  mean that the Liberal Democrats will  effectively be subsumed into another  party no we're not subsumed into  anything but I don't know exactly what  form this could take it partly depends  on the events of the next few weeks  actually around breaks it could be a  coalition not standing against each  other it's not the kind of thing you're  talking about well they're all kind of  possibilities but I think where there  are common values we will operate  together it's very difficult since  that's up a new party under the  first-past-the-post system I think they  recognize that and it will be sensible  if we do have common values and common  interests that we work together in some  way but but that's somewhere down the  track how many MPs are we talking about  I don't know the figure of six this  morning seemed very much at the lower  end of the fingers quoted to me but  again we'll see it's that's their choice  not mine  okay thank you very much

